RETURNPARM02&colon.my second value
:ehp2.
:EXMP.
:p.The value from the line that starts with RETURNPARM01&colon. is returned in RETURN01, the value from
RETURNPARM02&colon.
is returned in RETURN02, and so on. The variables are set to blanks before the command runs. If a keyword is
not written to STDOUT the matching variable stays blank.
:p.:hp3.Note::ehp3. The command must be run from a CL program because the values are returned in CL
variables.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-variable:epv.
:pd.Specify the name of a character CL variable with a length of 255.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHGETPR2/RETURN10'.
Return parm 10 (255) (RETURN10) - Help
:xh3.Return parm 10 (255) (RETURN10)
:p.Specifies a CL variable that receives a value that was written to STDOUT by the command or script that
ran.
Up to 10 values can be returned. To return a value, the command or script writes a line to STDOUT that
starts with the keyword :hp2.RETURNPARMxx&colon.:ehp2. followed by the value, where xx is 01 to 10.
:XMP.
:hp2.
RETURNPARM01&colon.my first value
RETURNPARM02&colon.my second value
:ehp2.
:EXMP.
:p.The value from the line that starts with RETURNPARM01&colon. is returned in RETURN01, the value from
RETURNPARM02&colon.
is returned in RETURN02, and so on. The variables are set to blanks before the command runs. If a keyword is
not written to STDOUT the matching variable stays blank.
:p.:hp3.Note::ehp3. The command must be run from a CL program because the values are returned in CL
variables.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-variable:epv.
:pd.Specify the name of a character CL variable with a length of 255.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHGETPR2/WRTDIAGMSG'.
Write return parms to joblog (WRTDIAGMSG) - Help
:xh3.Write return parms to joblog (WRTDIAGMSG)
:p.Specifies whether the return values are written to the job log as messages.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*NO:epk.
:pd.The return values are not written to the job log.
:pt.:pk.*YES:epk.
:pd.The return values are written to the job log.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHGETPR2/WRTDTAARA'.
Write return parms to dtaara (WRTDTAARA) - Help
:xh3.Write return parms to dtaara (WRTDTAARA)
:p.Specifies whether the return values are written to data areas. Ten character data areas of length 255
named RTNPARM01 to RTNPARM10 are created in the library named by DTAARALIB if they do not exist.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*NO:epk.
:pd.The return values are not written to data areas.
:pt.:pk.*YES:epk.
:pd.The return values are written to data areas RTNPARM01 to RTNPARM10.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHGETPR2/DTAARALIB'.
Library for temp data areas (DTAARALIB) - Help
:xh3.Library for temp data areas (DTAARALIB)
:p.Specifies the library the RTNPARM01 to RTNPARM10 data areas are created in when WRTDTAARA(*YES) is used.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.QTEMP:epk.
:pd.The data areas are created in library QTEMP, so they only exist for the life of the job.
:pt.:pv.library-name:epv.
:pd.Specify the name of the library for the data areas.
:eparml.
:ehelp.

.************************************************************************
.*  Primary Help for command QSHPYCALL
.************************************************************************
:help name='QSHPYCALL'.
Run Python and Return Parms (QSHPYCALL) - Help
:p.The Run Python and Return Parms (QSHPYCALL) command runs a Python script with the QSHEXEC command and
returns up to 10 values to CL variables. It is meant for CL, RPG and COBOL programmers who are used to
calling
programs and getting values back through parameters. The values come from lines that the Python script
writes
to STDOUT that start with RETURNPARM01&colon. to RETURNPARM10&colon..
:p.The command must be run from a CL program because it returns values in CL variables. A sample script,
pycallparm1.py, is in the qshpython directory of the QshOni project.
:p.The following example calls a Python script and returns one value:
:XMP.
:hp2.
DCL VAR(&amp.RETURN01) TYPE(*CHAR) LEN(255)
QSHPYCALL SCRIPTDIR('/qshpython')
          SCRIPTFILE('pycallparm1.py')
          ARGS('--parm1=inputvalue1')
          PYVERSION(3)
          DSPSTDOUT(*NO)
          RETURN01(&amp.RETURN01)
:ehp2.
:EXMP.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/LOGGINGNOTE'.
:P.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/ASTERISK'.
:ehelp.

:help name='QSHPYCALL/SCRIPTDIR'.
Python script directory (SCRIPTDIR) - Help
:xh3.Python script directory (SCRIPTDIR)
:p.Specifies the IFS directory that contains the Python script.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.Enter the IFS directory path. For example /qshpython.
:eparml.
:P.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/REQPARM'.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/SCRIPTFILE'.
Python script file name (SCRIPTFILE) - Help
:xh3.Python script file name (SCRIPTFILE)
:p.Specifies the Python script file name without the directory path. The command puts the directory and
file name together.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.Enter the script file name. For example hello.py.
:eparml.
:P.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/REQPARM'.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/ARGS'.
Python command line arguments (ARGS) - Help
:xh3.Python command line arguments (ARGS)
:p.Specifies the command line arguments that are passed to the Python script. Up to 40 arguments of up to
200 characters each can be passed. Each argument is trimmed automatically.
:p.Do not put double quotes around the arguments unless you use ARGDLM(*NONE), because the command adds the
delimiter for you and extra quotes can change the value that the script receives.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.Enter the argument value.
:eparml.
:P.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/LIST'.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/ARGDLM'.
Command line arg delimiter (ARGDLM) - Help
:xh3.Command line arg delimiter (ARGDLM)
:p.Specifies the delimiter that is automatically placed around each command line argument value.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*DBLQUOTE:epk.
:pd.Double quotes are placed around each argument.
:pt.:pk.*SGLQUOTE:epk.
:pd.Single quotes are placed around each argument.
:pt.:pk.*NONE:epk.
:pd.No delimiter is added. Use this when you want to control exactly where quotes are placed in your
arguments.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/PYVERSION'.
Python version (PYVERSION) - Help
:xh3.Python version (PYVERSION)
:p.Specifies the Python version used to run the script.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.3:epk.
:pd.Python 3 is used.
:pt.:pk.2:epk.
:pd.Python 2 is used.
:pt.:pk.2.7:epk.
:pd.Python 2.7 is used.
:pt.:pk.3.6:epk.
:pd.Python 3.6 is used.
:pt.:pk.3.9:epk.
:pd.Python 3.9 is used.
:pt.:pk.3.13:epk.
:pd.Python 3.13 is used.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.Enter another Python version number.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/PYPATH'.
Python bin path (PYPATH) - Help
:xh3.Python bin path (PYPATH)
:p.Specifies the directory path to the Python binaries (python or python3).
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*DEFAULT:epk.
:pd.The path stored in the PYPATH data area in library QSHONI is used. If there is no value the standard
path /QOpenSys/pkgs/bin is used.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.Enter the full IFS path of the directory that contains the Python binaries. For example
/QOpenSys/pkgs/bin.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/SETPKGPATH'.
Set QOpenSys yum package path (SETPKGPATH) - Help
:xh3.Set QOpenSys yum package path (SETPKGPATH)
:p.Specifies whether to prepend /QOpenSys/pkgs/bin to PATH.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*YES:epk.
:pd.Include package path.
:pt.:pk.*NO:epk.
:pd.Do not include package path.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/USEVENV'.
Use virtual environment (USEVENV) - Help
:xh3.Use virtual environment (USEVENV)
:p.Specifies whether the script runs in an existing Python virtual environment. The virtual environment is
activated before the script runs and deactivated afterward.
:p.:hp3.Note::ehp3. Each virtual environment has its own Python executable. When a virtual environment is
used,
make sure the PYPATH value is blank so the system level Python is not used.
:parml.
:pt.:pk def.*NO:epk.
:pd.A virtual environment is not used.
:pt.:pk.*YES:epk.
:pd.The script runs in the virtual environment named by VENVPATH. The directory and the virtual
environment must exist.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/VENVPATH'.
Virtual environment base path (VENVPATH) - Help
:xh3.Virtual environment base path (VENVPATH)
:p.Specifies the IFS path of the Python virtual environment. This is only used when USEVENV(*YES) is
specified.
:parml.
:pt.:pv.character-value:epv.
:pd.Enter the base path of the virtual environment.
:eparml.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/DSPSTDOUT'.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/DSPSTDOUT'.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/LOGSTDOUT'.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/LOGSTDOUT'.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/PRTSTDOUT'.
:IMHELP NAME='COMMON/PRTSTDOUT'.
:ehelp.
:help name='QSHPYCALL/DLTSTDOUT'.
